.TH n3n-supernode 8  "1 Oct 2026" "version 3" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
n3n-supernode \- n3n supernode daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B n3n-supernode
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.br
.B n3n-supernode start
[\fIsessionname\fR]
.br
.B n3n supernode
[\fIoptions..\fR] [command] [\fIcommand args\fR]
.br
.B n3n-supernode --help
.SH DESCRIPTION
N3N is a peer-to-peer VPN system - it is the LAN you have when you have noLAN.

The Supernode is a node introduction registry, broadcast conduit and packet
relay node for the n3n system.  It is the supernode role of the
.BR n3n (8)
program, which also runs as
.BR "n3n supernode" .

Once it has the full config loaded, the supernode begins listening on the
addresses of \fBconnection.bind\fR, UDP and TCP, for node registrations, and
other packets to route.  By default that is [::]:7654, which stands for IPv6
and IPv4 on port 7654; several addresses can be given, and the supernode
answers edges on each of them alike.  An address with \fBudp://\fR or
\fBtcp://\fR in front is for that transport only, for example
\fBbind = udp://[::]:7654 tcp://[::]:443\fR; the edges then need to be told
of the TCP port, see \fBcommunity.supernode\fR in
.BR n3n-edge (8).  The
supernode can service any number of communities and routes packets only between
members of the same community. The supernode does not hold the community
encryption key and so cannot snoop or inject packets into the community.

Supernode can service a number of n3n communities concurrently. Traffic does
not cross between communities.

All logging goes to stdout, or with \fBdaemon.background\fR to syslog.

The configuration file is found as for the edge, by the session name
(default "supernode"): /etc/n3n/\fB<sessionname>\fR.conf.  All options are
described by \fBn3n-edge help config\fR and in docs/configure/Options.md.

.SH OPTIONS
Some of the most common options also have a shortcut version and you can see
all of them with:

.RS
.B n3n-supernode help options
.RE
.TP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-O \fR<\fIsection\fR>.<\fIoption\fR>=<\fIvalue\fR>
Sets any config option value.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\--help\fR
Show some quick usage notes.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\--version\fR
Show the version.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-daemon\fR
Fork and run as a daemon, instead of the default of running in foreground.
This is ignored on windows.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
increase logging verbosity

.SH ALLOWED COMMUNITIES FILE
Specified with \fB\-Osupernode.community_file=\fR option (or in a config file)
This file is a plain text file.  Comments are introduced with a hash at the
beginning of the line.  A line that begins with an asterisk is a user
authentication definition and adds an allowed user to the most recently defined
community.  Allowed communities can be specified with a regular expression.
.PP
Example community file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# List of allowed communities
mynetwork
netleo
* logan nHWum+r42k1qDXdIeH-WFKeylK5UyLStRzxofRNAgpG
* sister HwHpPrdMft+38tFDDiunUds6927t0+zhCMMkQdJafcC
.RE
.fi
.PP
More details on creating the allowed communities file are found in the
Communities.md and Authentication.md documentation included with this package.
.SH COMMUNITIES IN THE CONFIGURATION
The communities can also be given in the configuration file, a section for
each, or along with the community file; a section wins over the same
community in the file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[community netleo]
network = 10.77.0.0/24
user = logan nHWum+r42k1qDXdIeH-WFKeylK5UyLStRzxofRNAgpG

[supernode]
community_regex = myCommunity[0-9][0-9]
.RE
.fi
.PP
With any allowed communities given, the supernode takes no others.  A reload
(\fBn3nctl reload_communities\fR) reads the community file again; a
reload of the whole configuration (\fBn3nctl reload\fR, SIGHUP) takes in
changed \fB[community\fR \fIname\fR\fB]\fR sections too, see RELOAD.
.SH FEDERATION
Supernodes with the same \fBsupernode.federation\fR name form a federation:
each \fBsupernode.peer\fR is another one to register with.  Edges can use
any supernode of a federation and still reach each other, and move to another
one when theirs goes away.  See docs/configure/Federation.md.
.SH THE SUPERNODE AS AN EDGE
With \fBsupernode.tap\fR=true the supernode also joins \fBcommunity.name\fR
with a TAP device of its own, set up by the \fB[tuntap]\fR options as for an
edge.  It reaches the other edges of the community through itself.  It needs
the privileges to open the TAP device at start, before it drops them.  See
docs/configure/Supernode.md.
.SH MANAGEMENT INTERFACE
As the edge, the supernode answers JsonRPC requests and serves a web page on
the Unix Domain socket /run/n3n/\fB<sessionname>\fR/mgmt, and on the TCP port
\fBmanagement.port\fR of localhost.  With \fBsupernode.tap\fR, the page has a
section for its edge too, and \fBn3nctl -r edge\fR asks the edge.  See
docs/internals/ManagementAPI.md.
.SH THREADS
With \fBdaemon.threads\fR the supernode relays PACKETs on several threads.
This needs Linux and a build with \fB--enable-pthread\fR, see
docs/advanced/Threads.md.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B N3N_FEDERATION
set the federation name so it is not visible at the command line
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B n3n-supernode start -v
Start supernode listening on the default port (7654), IPv6 and IPv4, with
verbose output.
.TP
.B n3n-supernode start \-O connection.bind='[::]:7654 [::]:7655'
Also listen on port 7655.  With two ports, an edge can see that its NAT maps
every destination to a port of its own, even with just this supernode.
.PP
.SH RELOAD
\fBn3nctl reload\fR, \fBsystemctl reload\fR or SIGHUP (not while it runs
in a terminal, where SIGHUP stops it) has the supernode read its
configuration again.  The communities, \fBlogging.verbose\fR and
\fBmanagement.password\fR change at once; other changes are logged as
waiting for a restart.  See docs/configure/ConfigurationFiles.md.
.SH RESTART
When supernode restarts it loses all registration information from associated
edge nodes.  The edges register again within their
\fBconnection.register_interval\fR (20 seconds by default); an edge that has
another supernode of the federation moves there when its supernode does not
answer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
supernode is a daemon and any exit is an error
